{
  "title": "Exertional Heat Illnesses",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/knee-and-sports/3117/exertional-heat-illnesses",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Mar 20 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Exertional heat illness is a broad category of injuries related to heat gains exceeding heat losses",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Any athlete or laborer who collapses or has a mental status change should undergo prompt medical evaluation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Exertional heat stroke is a life-threatening condition that requires immediate whole-body cooling, IV rehydration, and EMS activation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The incidence of exertional heat illnesses rises during the warmer months of the year",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Highest incidence with American football, running, cycling, and adventure races",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Exertional heat illnesses arise when body heat gain exceeds heat loss",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": ".Predisposing factors include inappropriate heat acclimation, dehydration, sleep deprivation, and prior heat illness",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation and Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Exertional heat exhaustion",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Inability to continue activity due to heat stress, with widespread peripheral vascular dilation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Core temperature of less than 102.2°F(39°C) and an absence of central nervous system dysfunction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Presents with profuse body sweat, nausea, and vomiting",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Treatment: body cooling, rehydration (oral preferred), continued monitoring until symptoms improve",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Exertional Heat Stroke",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Hyperthermia (core temp>102.2°F or 40°C), CNS dysfunction, multiple organ system failure",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "A medical emergency with a high death rate that results from failure of the thermoregulatory mechanisms of the body",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Immediate recognition is paramount to survival. Rectal temp >40°C and any CNS change necessitates body cooling",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Immediate whole-body cooling on-site (cold water immersion), prior to evac (unless other medical complications)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "IV resuscitation with treatment of secondary medical complications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Rapid cooling that lowers body temp within an hour of onset typically results in full recovery",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Exertional rhabdomyolysis",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Acute syndrome of muscle destruction producing breakdown product leakage into the bloodstream",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Presents with muscle pain, weakness, elevated serum creatine kinase, and myoglobinuria",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Treated with body cooling if necessary, plus aggressive IV rehydration and potential renal dialysis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Exertional associated collapse",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Collapse of a conscious athlete with or without syncope after completion of an exercise",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Caused by loss of venous return at the end of an exercise",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Most common condition seen in a marathon medical tent (59-85%)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Rule out other serious injuries, then oral hydration and Trendelenburg position",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Exercise associated muscle cramps",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Painful skeletal muscle spasm secondary to electrolyte imbalance",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Affected muscles can be random, individual spams usually lasts 1-3min",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Treatment is stretching and oral rehydration with combo NaCl",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Exercise associated hyponatremia",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Potentially life threatening condition with serum sodium<135 mg/dl and mental status change",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Usually due to dilutional hyponatremia from prolonged ingestion (>3hrs) of hypotonic fluids",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Presents with mental status change, peripheral paresthesias, and extremity swelling",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Severe cases treated with immediate EMS evac and IV hypertonic saline",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Management",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "5",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Rapid evaluation of any athlete who collapses or has a mental status change during or shortly after an event",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Immediate whole-body cooling (cold water immersion) should be available at high-risk events",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Prevention",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Heat acclimatization, gradual increase in activity with heat exposure",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Activity modification in high-risk environments",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Wet bulb temperature",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "temperature read off a thermometer wrapped in a wet cloth",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "82 - 86 degrees: athletes should have a 1:1 practice to rest ratio",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "> 86 degrees: sports participation should be postponed",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Return to play",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "No evidence-based recommendations for return to play after exertional heat stroke",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Recommendation to rest for 7 days, then gradually increase over the next month. Cleared for competition if heat tolerance returns",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
